; Label's VERTICAL alignment offset — the one number `vertical_alignment`
; decides, and the only thing that varies across these five panes.
;
; Every pane sets the SAME text in the SAME font size (16) at the SAME
; `horizontal_alignment` (left, the default, whose offset is a constant 0 for
; Label's empty style box) inside a box of the SAME width. Only
; `vertical_alignment` and the box HEIGHT change, so any row a glyph lands on
; that the engine does not agree with is attributable to the vertical offset
; alone.
;
; Why a regression here is invisible in every other scene: every other Label,
; Button, CheckBox and RichTextLabel fixture in the set leaves
; `vertical_alignment` at its default VERTICAL_ALIGNMENT_TOP, where the offset
; is identically zero and the three other branches are dead code. `label-wrap`
; is the only other scene with multi-line text, and it is TOP as well, so its
; per-line pitch never carries the FILL branch's extra separation either.
;
; The panes, and what each one pins:
;
;   TopRef       100 px box, TOP    — the zero-offset reference row. Every
;                                     other pane's offset is read as its own
;                                     ink row minus this one's, which cancels
;                                     the rasteriser's own glyph shape.
;   CenterWhole   99 px box, CENTER — box minus content is even, so halving it
;                                     lands on a whole pixel by itself.
;   CenterHalf   100 px box, CENTER — box minus content is odd, so halving it
;                                     lands on exactly x.5. Godot keeps the
;                                     offset in an `int` (`label.cpp`,
;                                     `Label::get_layout_data`, `int vbegin =
;                                     0, vsep = 0`), so this pane's glyphs must
;                                     land on the SAME row as CenterWhole's
;                                     despite the 1 px taller box. A renderer
;                                     that keeps the half pixel splits the two
;                                     panes by a row.
;   BottomRef    100 px box, BOTTOM — its offset is box minus content with no
;                                     division at all, so its measured row
;                                     states the engine's own content height
;                                     directly, which is what makes the two
;                                     CENTER panes' halving readable as a
;                                     halving rather than as a disagreement
;                                     about how tall the text is.
;   FillFour     205 px box, FILL   — four lines, so the offset is a per-gap
;                                     separation applied three times. 104/3
;                                     has a fractional part of two thirds,
;                                     which accumulates to a full 2 px by the
;                                     last line: this branch's error is a
;                                     whole-row displacement, not a
;                                     half-pixel argument.
;
; Each pane's grey plate is exactly its Label's own rect, so an offset reads
; against the plate's top and bottom edges rather than against a guess.
;
; ENGINE rows, measured against Godot 4.6.3 at `--mode 2d` — first inked row
; per pane, and the offset each one implies:
;
;   TopRef        30      offset 0 — the reference row
;   CenterWhole   68      offset 38
;   CenterHalf    68      offset 38, the same row from a 1 px taller box
;   BottomRef    107      offset 77, i.e. a content height of 23
;   FillFour     186, 246, 306, 366 — a flat 60 px pitch, i.e. a separation of
;                         34 on top of the 26 px line pitch
;
; What this scene pins is the OFFSET column, which is identical on both sides.
; The absolute rows above are the engine's own and are not the golden's: the
; previewer draws these glyphs from an MSDF atlas rather than through Godot's
; rasteriser, and its ink box for a line of this slice's text runs one row
; taller at the top (Label's comparison sheet carries that measurement for the
; other two Label scenes). So a golden whose ink starts a row above every
; number here is that same standing atlas difference; a golden whose
; pane-to-pane DIFFERENCES are not 0 / 38 / 38 / 77 and 60 / 60 / 60 is a
; vertical-alignment regression.

